        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Cardiac autonomic function and electrical stability will be better preserved in artificial gravity (AG-treated) subjects than in head down bed rest (HDBR) controls.
<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
Supine high-fidelity 12-lead ECG recordings were done for 5 minute durations at 3 time points in this study: Pre-bedrest (BR-07), and post-bedrest (BR+0, BR+4).  Breathing was controlled (15 breaths/min = 0.25 Hz).  The Mason-Likar electrode configuration was used, as was a sampling rate of 1000 samples/s/channel.  An expanded amplifier bandwidth (300 Hz low pass) was used.<P>

Electrocardiographic Methods:  A conventional ECG was used, plus multiple advanced ECG parameters were measured via specialized software (Cardiosoft).  Beat-to-beat heart rate variability (HRV, multiple stochastic & deterministic types) was measured. Other measurements included: beat-to-beat QT interval variability (QTV, with application of SVD to QTV), T-wave morphology by singular value decomposition (SVD) using signal averaging, “SAECG” (including “late potentials” via 12-lead-to-Frank (XYZ) transform), spatial mean QRS/ventricular gradients, spatial QRS-T angles, and other left ventricular mass parameters.  High frequency QRS ECG (150-250 Hz) using signal average was done. Estimates of baroreflex sensitivity (BRS) using peak-to-trough excursions in RR intervals during slow breathing (6 breaths/min=0.1 Hz) without a beat-to-beat BP device.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
Many parameters showed a prominent “day effect”, i.e., effect of head down bed rest (HDBR) itself, but with no “day x group” effect (i.e., no difference in the response between groups).  For example, in both groups on BR+0 compared to pre-HDBR, the following parameters showed this effect: (1)Heart rate (2) “Sympathetic” HRV indices [LF/(LF+HF); LF/HF of HRV], ± _QTc interval (mild) and (3)  “Parasympathetic” HRV indices and parameters, _“LV mass” parameters by ECG.
<P>
A relevant “day x group” effect (i.e., effect of countermeasure) was found only for (1) “Mixed” HRV parameters (but only when BR+04 result also included in the ANOVA), (2) QT Variability Index (_QTVI), and (3) possibly for “estimated BRS”.  
<P>
Brief daily AG treatment by short-radius centrifuge during each of 21 days of HDBR does not appear to protect against HDBR-related losses of supine cardiac autonomic function nor of “LV mass” as estimated by ECG, perhaps due to a hydration effect. 
<P>
One exception to this may be that the AG treatment may have helped to better preserve “baroreflex sensitivity” as estimated by the 0.1 Hz breathing technique, based strictly on the group-related difference on BR+0 (albeit no day x group effect).  One potential concern is the greater increase in QTVI in the AG group compared to the HDBR-control group—i.e., on BR+0, with further worsening on BR+04. This finding may hint at the possibility of creating an autonomic nervous system milieu that might eventually be more arrhythmogenic in AG-treated groups.
<P>
Therefore, the AG countermeasure appears to be potentially helpful for orthostatic tolerance (i.e., estimated BRS + tilt data).  It is not helpful, or may even be potentially harmful, for cardiac electrical stability (i.e., QTVI data). Several autonomic parameters, including BRS, can be influenced by hydration status. Thus, it will be important in future experiments to correlate the HDBR-induced changes in cardiac autonomic parameters with those in plasma volume.  Similarly, ECG-based measures of LV mass might actually reflect LV volumes (and/or the volume of LV muscle, which includes water). Thus, the significant “day” effect observed on ECG-based “LV mass” parameters in this study may relate to decrements in plasma volume, and/ to changes in volume within the LV cavity or LV myocardium itself. Results should thus ultimately be corroborated by the echo results obtained in this study (although echo has some similar limitations) or ideally by cardiac MRI in future studies. 
<P>
Note that consistent with the findings of Buchheit et al (AJP Heart Circ Physiol 291, 451-58, 2006), in the present study, there was also a strong correlation between VO2max and resting baseline “parasympathetic” HRV parameters. For example, on BR-07 in this study, the correlation between VO2max and HF/(HF+LF) was R = 0.62, p = 0.035.
        </div>

